## Why This Is Asked

This question probes ability to design a real-time, privacy-preserving streaming system with strict latency, security, and auditability requirements. It tests how a candidate handles continuous data ingestion, secure aggregation in a streaming window, dynamic key rotation, and robust testing under adversarial conditions.

## Key Concepts

- Real-time streaming with windowed aggregation
- Online differential privacy and secure aggregation
- Key rotation and attestation for TEEs
- Replay protection and auditable provenance
- Burst handling, clock skew, and fault tolerance

## Code Example

```javascript
// Lightweight sketch of stream_submit handler
function handleStreamSubmit(req, res) {
  const { device_id, region, ts, window_id, score_share, signature } = req.body;
  // verify signature, map to window, apply masking
  res.status(202).send({ accepted: true, window_id });
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you enforce per-window privacy budgets across tenants and handle overages?
- How would late-arriving shares affect the current window and what reprocessing strategy would you adopt?

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes a candidate's ability to design a robust, privacy-preserving streaming system with practical API design, data modeling, and real-world constraints (late arrivals, auditing, fault tolerance).

## Key Concepts

- Secure aggregation in streaming systems
- Tamper-evident append-only logging
- Late-arrival handling and fault tolerance
- Differential privacy budgeting in continuous streams
- API design for edge-device contributions

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code: verify and accumulate a batch of shares securely
function processBatch(batch){
  const maskedSum = batch.shares.reduce((a,s)=>a + s.maskedValue,0);
  // server-side de-masking happens with stored keys
  return unmask(maskedSum);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you rotate device keys without interrupting service?
- How would you detect and mitigate poisoned shares in real time?
